#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "a19f597d-f584-57b3-9e67-cb4cea07c876" :question/math-academy-id "e-21120" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "83ebd59d-883f-510a-9bc9-035b3088a43c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21123" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/63/630f2ae400ab4ccd212138703f93516384c23b284f5ecac5b8c459964f436d0b.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/ff/fffb7d52ff1ec576d336b3498e40ea433546d971a398550848359cda657f957c.svg)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](images/d5/d5c965f4aa3a43689e92d60295049b1d7388c0d7eaf8b8ee93c1bd7eec4af207.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/e4/e4b23c56134ac2b5f813ebbe4aa89f4ca4fc35a9c96be1471b4d37915270e46f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f5136df2-fcf6-5f64-aeda-f14a91231788" :question/math-academy-id "e-21124" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/11/11d3e9693d6aa5f2b7836f4efad788fc8693d8e28ed2f0d8b5bf3ea2b9937850.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)})}^{t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0).$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413590} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad62917-fe79-54d5-9e2a-eb52a863023e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413593} {:answer/id #uuid "a5551b66-4909-5792-8d3d-3a30ec491dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413596} {:answer/id #uuid "b09a08e9-f214-5a5c-b110-f1010c4b03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413598} {:answer/id #uuid "c068b9da-8858-56ed-90bd-f563b1b66009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ef9340-52d9-5cb4-999f-d1140490bfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413590} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22c5a1e-381b-53d5-9e61-1f0e13b8d114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96f65a49-2a61-5981-a404-aa212d4de13c" :question/math-academy-id "q-331097" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "276898e7-57a4-5ca6-8df7-99fd318a2702" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Decoupled System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4677b7a7-7f61-5a88-a5ee-a34ee62c287d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413589} {:answer/id #uuid "91bf1bed-82dd-5957-bd20-b0c4787407e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413594} {:answer/id #uuid "96da30d2-eb7e-540b-b235-30c2de13031a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413595} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2050e5-3099-5de8-9248-cf99c56b8941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413597} {:answer/id #uuid "c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c90c80ee-ce2e-5334-9b0a-1654558fcf8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413600} :answer-field/id #uuid "3adf9e22-bc52-5ded-9787-739dfa3cf328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac9ae842-1c73-58e9-9ed3-756e4fa56ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-331980" :question/problem "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.  \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bf251b-dca8-56d7-a92a-48dd1eb46b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302461} {:answer/id #uuid "13696ed9-764e-541a-8b04-7b7e4287c673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302584} {:answer/id #uuid "45ae77fa-b6af-5c75-b8a3-fa4d197cfa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302896} {:answer/id #uuid "b263b137-d143-5ff8-a8a1-4f137bca92cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303560} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0e3b76-73a4-5a6b-914c-d61f970f4a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ae77fa-b6af-5c75-b8a3-fa4d197cfa59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302896} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d97aabb-32f0-532f-a52d-830e1ddabbe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69caff5b-f7ed-5470-8207-7540a07ed223" :question/math-academy-id "q-331989" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e380f8b-d71f-55a2-ab1b-39f34cd89e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391052} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9827f0-71cf-5847-85bc-980bee16161f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391053} {:answer/id #uuid "ca29bc6f-4907-5a9b-91bf-39c3f6d764fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391054} {:answer/id #uuid "b312d8e4-025a-5e6d-99d6-bb6fe41ba80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391055} {:answer/id #uuid "755f8ee4-1bac-5f8b-adc2-777c045fa529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b312d8e4-025a-5e6d-99d6-bb6fe41ba80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391055} :answer-field/id #uuid "6964b2cb-9fd8-5726-b5f5-384b2f89e701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5620c47b-4e97-5ced-9381-5d5cc172acfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332709" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{8t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\sqrt{5}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce35e5a9-2444-5e5f-9463-5a4d54496d9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Decoupled Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abeb303-65a1-580a-8d0c-2e6a1c960b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413587} {:answer/id #uuid "18398e71-d597-5623-af51-1629bf876027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413588} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9de9ca-9cf2-557a-8cdb-19ad0d5e57b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413591} {:answer/id #uuid "69db9e77-56bd-5ad8-9ac5-e356be91510d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413592} {:answer/id #uuid "f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f322c546-6f3c-57e2-a376-310fe63163d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413601} :answer-field/id #uuid "56cd2b18-0b66-5513-b0b3-8386a57997dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "983f8128-2e0d-5ae8-ba5b-ec2151df4bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39026" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{3}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{3}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cec0e48f-9fbb-5ada-b7fd-0d389d1b7438" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for a Decoupled Linear System of First-Order ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9d9a47-3583-59c2-a4a5-d9b7cfbabc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392287} {:answer/id #uuid "932c6061-7cb9-580f-acce-6047383bf69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392288} {:answer/id #uuid "690d1f44-18ea-5111-91c1-cd804b8318f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392289} {:answer/id #uuid "3046ed62-20a1-55c9-81f9-f2e7be7aafa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392290} {:answer/id #uuid "820e88f3-13a8-52c4-9120-9c26a28538cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e9d9a47-3583-59c2-a4a5-d9b7cfbabc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392287} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd8613b-a6de-5527-afe3-03ff9a9e544b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e051aa-62ce-5cb4-9eb2-83e2d1aaa8ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39269" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
